{"step_index":1840,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T13:30:31Z","input_tokens":2320,"cache_read_tokens":213651,"output_tokens":644,"content":"That is the true superpower of this architecture. Because the **OSCAL taxonomy is centralized into its own registry plugin** and automatically injected into every plugin's schema via `ensure_category_metadata_fields(&mut schema)`:\n\n1. **Inherited by Default**:\n   * No plugin author has to hand-craft compliance metadata.\n   * Every single method (`mut.*`, `obs.*`, `src.*`) automatically inherits the standardized category metadata fields, actor tracking, and validation rules.\n2. **Dynamic & Customizable**:\n   * Changing, extending, or tightening the compliance rules in `oscal_subid_registry` immediately cascades across the entire system catalog and all 74 plugins on the next seal.\n\n### The Ethereum Pattern Will Work the Exact Same Way:\n\nWe apply this exact inheritance pattern to **on-chain contract anchoring**:\n\n* **A Centralized Contract / Attestation Registry**:\n  A dedicated plugin (e.g. `eth_registry` or `attestation_registry`) tracks supported contract ABIs, chain IDs (e.g. Arbitrum, Sepolia), and settlement rules.\n* **Inherited Attestation Envelopes**:\n  Just like `ensure_category_metadata_fields(&mut schema)` injects OSCAL subids, a helper like `ensure_contract_attestation_fields(&mut schema)` automatically injects:\n  * `attestation_digest`\n  * `chain_id`\n  * `contract_address`\n  into any mutating plugin method that triggers real-world contract settlement.\n* **Result**:\n  Any plugin—whether it's `cron`, `workflows`, `cognitive`, or `identity_sled`—can trigger on-chain contract settlement without needing bespoke Web3 code. It simply inherits the contract taxonomy from the registry!\n\nIt keeps the entire system modular, uniform, and cleanly expandable as you build out the hackathon project over the next 10 days.","thinking":"Every plugin automatically integrates category metadata fields through a shared function call, ensuring consistency. The taxonomy is centrally defined and inherited, confirming the user's observation that the OSCAL subvolume taxonomy is both customizable and inherited across plugins. This design enables a unified and extensible taxonomy system.\n\n\n"}
